    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the proximal tibia. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy", "Primary radiation therapy followed by resection", "Surgical resection alone", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
