    (v_topic_id, 'A 53-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal tibia. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the humerus that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Cryoablation without imaging", "Radiation therapy", "Open en bloc resection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 40-year-old patient presents with a slowly growing, deep, painless mass in the popliteal fossa. MRI shows a heterogeneous mass near the knee joint, but not within the joint capsule. Biopsy confirms a soft tissue sarcoma. Genetic testing reveals a t(X;18) chromosomal translocation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Clear cell sarcoma", "Synovial Sarcoma", "Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma", "Liposarcoma", "Rhabdomyosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Despite its name, Synovial Sarcoma rarely arises from the actual synovium of the joint, but typically occurs near large joints (e.g., the knee) in young adults. It is strongly associated with the t(X;18) translocation, which creates the SS18-SSX fusion gene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the radius. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 27-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the pelvis that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Cryoablation without imaging", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Radiation therapy", "Open en bloc resection", "Intra-articular steroid injection"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal tibia. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Bisphosphonates", "Denosumab", "Amputation", "Radiation therapy", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 63-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the pelvis. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 24-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the proximal femur that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Intravenous bisphosphonates", "Radiation therapy", "Chemotherapy followed by limb-salvage surgery", "Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Wide surgical resection"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal humerus. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
